# THE SLACK/SHARE PAIRS THAT CAN BE COMPARED AT ALL. One row per (layer, buffer, holder kind)
# where BOTH the slack and the share state a unit.
#
# ⭐⭐ A SLACK IS COMPARED AGAINST HOLDER SHARES, SO IT HAS TO BE IN THE SAME UNIT -- and a
#    pair where either side declines to say is not a mismatch, it is an unanswerable
#    question. Making the population "both stated" rather than "all pairs" is the difference
#    between a rule that examines something and a rule that reports on rows it cannot read.
# ⛔⛔⛔ IT IS THE ABSORBING BUFFER'S SLACK, NOT EVERY SLACK IN THE LAYER, AND THAT NARROWING IS
#    THE POINT OF THE FILE. This used to join entries/slacks.sqlc on (filing, layer) alone, so
#    every holder was compared against all three of its layer's buffers. Two thirds of the pairs
#    were a holder against a buffer that absorbs nothing of theirs, where a differing unit is no
#    defect at all -- and checks/slack_unit_mismatch.sqlc, whose sentence is 'the unit of the
#    shares IT BOUNDS', reported all of them as its examined population.
#
# ⭐ arithmetic/roster.sqlc names that rule the guard for `shares_bounded`, and that site joins
#   the absorber. A guard wider than the site it guards is not a stricter guard; it is a guard
#   answering a different question and counting the answer toward its own coverage.
-- entries/absorbing_slack.sqlc against pm:Remainder/pm:holder, each carrying its own unit.
SELECT s.filing, s.layer, s.buffer, h.kind,
       s.unit AS slack_unit, h.share_unit
FROM      (
    :compose(entries/absorbing_slack.sqlc)
) s
JOIN      (
    :compose(entries/holders.sqlc)
) h USING (filing, layer)
WHERE s.unit IS NOT NULL AND h.share_unit IS NOT NULL
